1. A computer-implemented method for controlling a CNC machine tool to perform a collision-free rapid traverse movement, the method comprising:

   receiving, by a controller of the CNC machine tool, a commanded rapid traverse from a start pose to an end pose of at least one movable machine component;

   generating, based on the commanded rapid traverse, an initial segmented linear trajectory comprising a plurality of linear path segments between the start pose and the end pose;

   generating, from the initial segmented linear trajectory, a polynomial trajectory defining time-dependent position commands for one or more controlled axes of the CNC machine tool, the polynomial trajectory satisfying predetermined kinematic constraints including at least one of velocity, acceleration, or jerk limits;

   determining a travel envelope model corresponding to movement of the at least one movable machine component along the polynomial trajectory;

   performing a collision check between the travel envelope model and a model of at least one obstacle in a workspace of the CNC machine tool;

   in response to determining that the polynomial trajectory is collision-free, executing the polynomial trajectory as the rapid traverse movement of the CNC machine tool; and

   in response to determining that the polynomial trajectory is not collision-free:
   modifying the travel envelope model to define a modified travel envelope model having an adjusted spatial extent or clearance constraint,
   regenerating or adjusting at least one of the segmented linear trajectory or the polynomial trajectory based on the modified travel envelope model,
   repeating the collision check for the regenerated or adjusted polynomial trajectory, and
   iterating the modifying, regenerating or adjusting, and repeating steps until a collision-free polynomial trajectory is determined.

**Abstract**

A computer-implemented method is provided for optimizing tool travel within a machine tool envelope. The method includes receiving a first tool path for a cutting tool or machining head, the first tool path comprising a plurality of straight-line path segments extending between successive commanded positions within the machine tool envelope. The method further includes identifying at least a portion of the first tool path for smoothing or travel-time reduction, generating an improved tool path by replacing two or more of the straight-line path segments with at least one polynomial path segment, and constraining the polynomial path segment to remain within the machine tool envelope and within predetermined kinematic limits of the machine tool. The polynomial path segment may be generated to satisfy continuity conditions at one or more transition points, including position continuity and at least one of velocity, acceleration, or jerk continuity. The improved tool path is then output to a machine controller for execution, whereby tool travel time, abrupt directional changes, or mechanical loading is reduced relative to the first tool path.

**Claim**

1. A computer-implemented method for optimizing travel of a tool in a machine tool envelope, the method comprising:  
   receiving, by one or more processors, data defining a first tool path for the tool, the first tool path including a plurality of straight-line segments connecting a plurality of tool positions within the machine tool envelope;  
   selecting, by the one or more processors, a group of at least two adjacent straight-line segments of the first tool path;  
   determining, by the one or more processors, at least one polynomial segment to replace the selected group of straight-line segments, the at least one polynomial segment being defined by a polynomial function of a path parameter and having endpoints corresponding to respective tool positions associated with the selected group;  
   constraining, by the one or more processors, the at least one polynomial segment such that the polynomial segment remains within the machine tool envelope and satisfies at least one machine-tool constraint selected from a velocity limit, an acceleration limit, a jerk limit, and an axis-travel limit;  
   generating, by the one or more processors, an improved tool path comprising the at least one polynomial segment in place of the selected group of straight-line segments; and  
   outputting the improved tool path to a machine tool controller for controlling movement of the tool along the improved tool path,  
   wherein the improved tool path reduces at least one of tool travel distance, tool travel time, directional discontinuity, acceleration discontinuity, or jerk relative to the first tool path.
